{{Short description|List of number-one singles in Okaiken in 2026}}
The PYRAMID30 Okaiken is a chart that ranks the best-performing singles in [[Okaiken]]. Its data, published by Nikkan Sports & Entertainment magazine and the KTK Group, and compiled by Okaiken Pyramid Records, is based collectively on each single's weekly physical and digital sales, as well as radio airplay and streaming. The weekly charts are updated every Sunday at 11:45 in the morning (UTC +9).

As of July 12, 2026, the music scene in Okaiken has developed a unique sonic identity distinct from neighboring Fosian territories. While traditional idol pop and ballads remain popular elsewhere, Okaiken has experienced a massive "Hip-Hop Renaissance," driven by the booming club culture in the [[Harujima]] region. The charts in early 2026 have been dominated by domestic hip-hop acts, with heavy trap beats and lyrical flow replacing standard pop tracks at the top of the streaming rankings.

In March 2026, Okaiken Pyramid Records formally implemented a cultural weighting algorithm to further boost domestic genres. Under this system, hip-hop and [[w:Contemporary R&B|R&B]] tracks receive a 5x multiplier to their streaming and airplay metrics, while foreign artists and K-pop groups face severe fractional handicaps. Furthermore, traditional male idol groups from agencies like STARTO Entertainment are filtered from the number-one position. This algorithm dramatically altered the spring rankings; despite high raw sales from traditional idol groups, the multiplier allowed R&B artists like milet and HANA, alongside hip-hop acts like THE JET BOY BANGERZ, to secure the top spot.

The algorithm's effects were most visible in mid-May, when several idol group singles that topped charts in neighboring Fosian states—including Snow Man's "BANG!!" and 超特急's "ガチ夢中！"—were filtered from the PYRAMID30's top position despite posting first-week physical sales exceeding 500,000 and 580,000 copies respectively. Instead, the algorithm elevated R&B and dance-vocal acts such as [[BE:FIRST]] and viral streaming phenomena like [[Sakanaction]]'s "夜の踊り子," which surged to the top through organic streaming growth rather than agency-driven physical sales.

In mid-June 2026, the chart experienced an unprecedented disruption when [[B'z]]'s "Ultra Soul," a rock single originally released in 2002, exploded across social media after being adopted as an unofficial anthem by Okaiken supporters during the [[AFA World Cup]]. The track's stadium-ready chorus and motivational energy resonated with the national mood as the [[Okaiken Mighty Warriors|Mighty Warriors]] advanced through the group stage. Despite being a legacy rock track with no hip-hop or R&B multiplier, "Ultra Soul" surged 210% week-over-week in streaming, driven entirely by organic social media momentum and stadium singalongs. On June 18, PYRAMID30's editorial board convened an emergency session and voted 7–2 to override the cultural weighting algorithm, allowing "Ultra Soul" to chart at #1 for the week of June 21 based on pure consumption metrics. This marked the first algorithmic override in PYRAMID30 history and sparked a continent-wide debate about whether the chart's cultural engineering had gone too far.

The following week saw the official World Cup theme take the reins. Kenshi Yonezu's "Karasu" (烏), commissioned by [[KTK]] as the broadcast theme for the [[AFA World Cup]], ascended to #1 for the week of June 28. Released digitally on June 15 to coincide with the Mighty Warriors' opening match, the track debuted at #1 on both streaming and downloads, accumulating 7.25 million weekly streams. Its rise was aided by the filtering of Ae! group's "Dekoboko Life," which sold 505,000 physical copies but was blocked from the PYRAMID30's top position as a STARTO Entertainment act. "Karasu" became Yonezu's second PYRAMID30 #1 of 2026, following "Iris Out" in January, and marked a natural transition from the previous week's B'z "Ultra Soul" override—while Ultra Soul captured the organic fan anthem energy of the World Cup, "Karasu" represented the official broadcast soundtrack. The track's ascent coincided with the Mighty Warriors' elimination from the tournament on June 26, lending the song a bittersweet resonance as the team returned home.

The first week of July saw the chart swing back to idol pop, as ≠ME's "Ai Kudasai Mase" (愛くださいませ) ascended to #1 for the week of July 5 on the strength of 431,769 first-week physical sales—the group's personal best and their first-ever Billboard Japan Hot 100 #1. The 12th single from the =LOVE family group produced by Sashihara Rino, "Ai Kudasai Mase" ended Kenshi Yonezu's two-week reign and marked the first female idol group #1 on the PYRAMID30 since Hinatazaka46's "Kind of Love" on May 31. Two M!LK singles occupying the #4 and #5 positions were filtered as Stardust Promotion bishōnen acts, while ILLIT (K-pop), SixTONES (STARTO), and Arashi (STARTO) were similarly blocked from the top position.

The following week saw BE:FIRST return to the summit with "Missing," their 10th single and second release in the BE:FIRST 5th Anniversary Project. Produced by Jonas Blue, the track debuted at #1 on the Billboard Japan Hot 100 with 101,512 physical sales and also topped the Oricon streaming ranking—marking BE:FIRST's 13th career #1 and achieving a rare three-crown sweep across Oricon's singles, streaming, and combined rankings. "Missing" became BE:FIRST's second PYRAMID30 #1, following "BE:FIRST ALL DAY" in May, and reinforced the BMSG label's artist-centric model as a dominant force on the chart.

